ough they may disagree about  politics what people value more is what  binds them together  as neighbors natasha name al jazeera  blue earth county minnesota well let's  now speak to jamie fly he's a senior  fellow at the german martial fund of the  united states and he was also  formerly an advisor to republican u.s  senator marco rubio he joins us  now from berlin jamie thanks for being  with us i do know for many people  outside the u.s it does seem crazy that  this whole election restaurant a very  small group of voters in just a few  states  and this time around a few traditionally  red states do seem to be leaning blue  do you think that's because of the  choice between trump and biden or  is it because we're seeing a changing  more diverse electorate  i think it's related to both those  factors as you noted  this election has generated passions on  both sides and so we have the potential  that we're not just dealing with  some of the traditional swing states  which have been contested now  over the last several decades places  like ohio  florida parts of the rust belt  but there are other states that people  are openly discussing possibly flipping  places like texas perhaps arizona and  some of that is just the energy  uh especially on the democratic side in  opposition  to president trump but some of it's also  related to changing demographics in a  number of those states where some of the  democratic party's core constituencies  are a growing segment of the electorate  and the democrats have invested a lot of  energy in this cycle  in actually trying to to draw those  voters to the polls  well two states that we're all going to  be watching very closely tonight florida  and pennsylvania especially given their  number of electoral college votes and  the polling margins that we're seeing  there  president trump is now already insisting  the counting of ballots beyond tonight  which  his standard practice is illegitimate do  you think this is all going to end 